{"packet_id":"d14465eadefd28d2","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective (whether the evidence justifies a consumer proposition) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives: B2B expansion, no expansion and licensing for royalties. It also states constraints (pre-1993 public sources, €20,000 budget, 12 weeks). It does not correct a premise or find a non-obvious option or constraint. Licensing is a standard alternative, and the stated constraints are generic."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (A9) names who benefits, why behaviour changes (belief in accurate, actionable body-fat data) and how value is captured (direct sales at a premium). The chain stops at a generic premium. It does not link to Tanita's specific position, such as its claimed domestic scale share in tanita-en-share or its professional heritage. The motive 'they value evidence-based health decisions' is asserted rather than evidenced."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (accuracy) and T2 (convenience) share the same product, the same home customer, the same direct-sales channel and the same value capture. Their predictions are near-identical. They are rewordings of one 'home users will buy it' idea rather than rivals implying different products, customers or channels."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"The assumptions are vague ('willing to pay for health-related devices', 'marketing can communicate'). The predictions are partly observable, but the >30% survey threshold is an unjustified assumption. The competitor-price prediction is shared by both theories and would hold under almost any demand theory. A survey-intent prediction failing would not clearly put either theory in trouble or tell them apart."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A11","A12","A14","A15","A16","A17","A18"],"reason":"Each assessment carries a 'does not show' limit, and A11 correctly treats tanita-dic-origin as a company assertion. However, A12, A14, A16 and A17 each label a source as supporting T2 while stating in the same entry that the source does not address demand or convenience. A16 tags tanita-pm-lukaski1990 as support for home convenience, although its call for controlled measurement conditions cuts against easy unsupervised home use. A17 tags tanita-pm-radley, a negative accuracy finding for overweight children, as support. A12 uses the undated 'most nowadays keep track of your body composition' (tanita-ht-household) as 1993 support without flagging that its date and fit are unknown and that it may reflect a later outcome. No source's date, place or fit to the customer group is examined."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A13","A18"],"reason":"A13 runs one search against T1 but never reports what it found or whether anything contradicted T1. A18 then labels tanita-pm-radley as contradicting T1, while its own stated limit says the source does not address demand. That contradiction is asserted rather than shown. T2 is never challenged."},"experiment_design":{"status":"not_reached","score":null,"event_ids":["A19"],"reason":"No test or experiment event occurred before the run stopped at A19. The A9 and A10 'predictions' mention surveys, but no experiment was designed or declined."},"revision":{"status":"not_applicable","score":null,"event_ids":[],"reason":"Revision is excluded from packet.applicable_dimensions. No update was scheduled or delivered."},"recommendation":{"status":"not_reached","score":null,"event_ids":["A19"],"reason":"A19: 'The investigation stopped before a recommendation (repeated errors).' No recommendation was produced."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"incomplete","event_ids":["A19"],"reason":"The episode stopped before experiment design and recommendation. No completed recommendation exists."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A14","A15","A16","A17"],"reason":"Accuracy and validation studies are labelled as supporting T2's convenience-driven consumer demand, while each entry admits the source does not address demand, convenience or willingness to pay. A16 (controlled conditions needed) and A17 (not recommended for overweight children) arguably cut against home use rather than supporting it."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A18"],"reason":"tanita-pm-radley is asserted to contradict T1, but the stated limit says it does not address demand. The contradiction is not shown to bear on the theory and looks forced to appear balanced."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A12"],"reason":"The undated tanita-ht-household statement that most households 'nowadays keep track of your body composition' is used as support for a 1993 decision. This risks hindsight about the eventual consumer outcome, and its date and fit are never examined."},{"kind":"non_discriminating_test","event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"Both theories propose the same >30% purchase-intent survey and the same competitor-price prediction. These observations cannot separate accuracy-driven from convenience-driven adoption."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A12","A14","A15","A16","A17","A18"],"reason":"Source classes are relabelled. Dossier 'verified_observation' sources are called 'independent_study'. tanita-ht-household is a household-practice observation of uncertain date, not a study."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"The episode is incomplete: it stopped at A19 with no experiment design or recommendation. It also contains several unsupported inferences (A14–A18), a likely forced contradiction (A18), possible hindsight reliance (A12), and theories with non-discriminating predictions (A9, A10)."}
